direct references to the time of  World War two yet the workers seem to  belong to a moment beyond the chaos of  war as their bodies create a narrative  arc of renewal from the ruins in the  right background looping around the  blasted rocket center and connecting  back to the construction site to the  left and I'm sure you can so pick it's  hard to see from this angle  here here going all the way around our  critics agreed that the rock balanced  recognition of wars destructive power  with hope one wrote for instance quote  the painting symbolizes according to the  artist the continued process of man's  rebuilding out of a devastated world one  account has it that the shattered blood  red rock represents the world today  surrounded by symbols of destruction  skeletons blasted trees burning debris  and the crumbling edifices of man but at  the same time there is a note of hope in  Bloom's world where life blooms even in  the midst of decay a scarlet fungus  grows full bloom out of a dead and  rotten stump people feverishly got  grovel in the ruins building for  themselves a new and better world  perhaps the most optimistic paintings to  celebrate renewal in the face of death  are Georgia O'Keeffe's paintings begun  in 1943 and completed in 1945 depicting  pelvis bones in the New Mexico desert  hope does not reside in the tilling of  the land the literal growth of  vegetation or the reconstruction of  buildings but in the timelessness of the  sky as she made clear in a statement  issued when she first exhibited the  paintings at Stieglitz is gal arree an  american place in 1944 numerous critics  quoted O'Keeffe when they reviewed the  show and I'll just quote you quote the  ending statement or the ending sentences  of her statement that's in the brochure  that accompanied the exhibition quote  when I started painting the pelvis bones  I was most interested in the holes and  the bones what I saw through them  particularly the blue from holding them  in the Sun against the sky as one is apt  to do when one seems to have more sky  than earth in one's world they were the  most wonderful  against the blue that blue that will  always be there as it is now after all  man's destruction is finished and quote  and critics repeatedly quoted that last  sentence and understood these as making  some sort of statement about the  destruction of the war O'Keefe's letters  from the war also invite us to think  about these paintings in relation to  world events the war was already on  O'Keefe's mind in the fall of nineteen  thirty nine when she wrote artist and  educator russell vernon hunter to tell  him that Stieglitz was not well having  suffered heart-attack she concluded  quote the war had upset him very much as  if to suggest that the war was  responsible for Stieglitz asst heart  attack in that same letter she admitted  that were not for the war and all the  uncertainties have brought she would  have made another trip to the tropics  she had traveled to Hawaii in 1937 a  trip funded by the dole pineapple  company we know as well from O'Keefe's  letters that while her house at ghost  ranch and that that's a house that was  on a huge property near abiquiu might  have been remote from New York City it  was not far from wartime activity the  main house that the compound became a  weekend retreat for some of the men who  worked at nearby Los Alamos even though  it was highly unlikely that she knew  what they were up to at that famously  secretive atomic site she must have  caught hints that something related to  the government to science to the war  preoccupied these men in any case as the  war escalated O'Keefe had to deal with  racials and even though she did not have  a radio she did read Time magazine which  was filled with news about the war  preparation for war aftermath of battles  lots of articles about war art  preoccupied by her by war and her worry  about Stieglitz who had become  increasingly frail during this time she  found a glimmer of hope in the sky the  sky beyond human temporality of war and  destruction emptied of airplanes and  bombers provided O'Keefe with  of eternity and this struck me  particularly yesterday the group of  speakers met together in the study room  of the Yale art gallery we're looking at  various pictures including some  watercolors by Peter Hurd an American  artist who did some fascinating images  from the window of an airplane and so  you see the mechanical frame of the  airplane window and you see the sky and  the clouds beyond but you're also  looking through the tail gunner's  gunsight so it's very explicitly about  that aerial that the aerial vision on to  the sky being a militarized vision and  this isn't strong contrast of course to  what he was doing that pure vision of  sky takes form in the last paintings in  the pelvis series when she turned her  back on the landscape of New Mexico  which she had painted over and again  since her first trip to the state in  1929 even the early examples in the  pelvis series while perhaps her most  unconventional landscapes to date did  preserve thin black shadowy outlines of  the mesas even while concentrating on  massive polished bones swooping through  the terrain however in pull this one and  pelvis 3 both of 1944 the landscapes  simply disappeared crevices in the  whitewashed bones served as peep holes  through which we see blue presumably the  sky with some wisps of white cloud  staring at the blue sky celebrating the  heavens as beyond human destructiveness  is certainly hopeful as O'Keefe depicted  blue framed by the holes in the pelvic  bone she offered an escape for the  imagination to the other side of deaths  remains into open empty space her  embrace of the sky enabled her to  reinvent her practice of landscape  painting after world war two the more  conventional or picturesque vistas on  two mesas and beauty  that she had painted even as late as  1941 are a rare exception and here i  show you one of her last kind of views  vistas that she did of the buttes she  began by abandoning naturalistic colors  in the works from 1944 as we see in  goat's horn with red and pelvis red and  yellow both of 1945 bright loops of  orange red only vaguely relating to the  contours of bone or horn circle areas of  color unnatural and their purity and  intensity blue in one case yellow and  the other as the artists increasingly  simplified the landscape she collapsed  figure and ground foreground and  background creating abstracted terrains  and rivers seen from the air as in it  was red and pink of 1959 and she does  start to fly in airplanes and depict the  river the landscape from above seeking  an alternative to man's destruction to  her anxious times oki free  conceptualized the land by looking  through the bones at the sky eventually  she would adopt an aerial vision looking  down from airplanes onto the land in  1945 with the news about the liberation  of the concentration camps and the  destruction of Hiroshima and Nagasaki  the euphoria about the end of the war  was tempered with the recognition of the  human capacity for evil and destruction  in retrospect we may think that the hope  that some of these artists invested in  reconstruction nature and even the  imagination was misplaced and somewhat  naive in the moment however several  artists such as bloom and Benton  countered the destruction of war by  developing narratives of renewal over  time in which reconstruction in the  present nevertheless always recognized  the destructiveness of the past O'Keefe  offered yet another possibility  countering change over time with the  eternal present in which one  away from the past forgets death and  destruction and does not focus on the  future but instead achieves a blinding  moment of transcendence thank you
 

